WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:04.650
When it comes to Dan Harmon and Justin Roiland's
weird and witty adult-centric animated series

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:08.160
Rick and Morty, you'd be forgiven for thinking
that just about anything goes.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:12.040
An entire storyline parodying Freddy Krueger
and the Nightmare on Elm Street franchise?

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.210
Yep, that happened.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.760
A character made from the DNA from Abraham
Lincoln and Adolf Hitler?

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:17.780
Yeah, that too.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:21.880
Whole episodes made up entirely of vignettes
from television series that only air in other

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:22.640
dimensions?

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:23.520
You betcha.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.340
"God-damn!"

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.480
Basically, fans of Rick and Morty are no strangers
to the bizarre.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:32.620
So it'd be easy to believe that practically
every idea the creative team pitches gets

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:33.120
accepted, no matter how bonkers it may be.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:34.340
But that isn't the truth.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.560
As it turns out, there was one idea the show's
creators had that never came to fruition — the

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:41.680
details of which remain a closely-guarded
secret to this day.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:42.940
"God-damn…"

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.320
Harmon and Roiland, who introduced Rick and
Morty to the world back in December 2013,

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.930
recently revealed to Entertainment Weekly
that they abandoned one episode of the show's

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:53.800
upcoming fourth season while it was still
in the planning phase.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:57.330
According to Harmon, the episode would have
needed a lot of elbow grease to be suitable

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:01.160
for mass consumption, and fixing its problems
would have taken far too long to be worth

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:01.660
doing.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:02.720
Harmon explained:

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:06.500
"Justin is going to kick me under the desk
here, but I've been pushing to show at Comic-Con

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.289
an animatic for an episode that we aborted
in the early stages.

00:01:09.289 --> 00:01:12.740
We could fix it, but fixing it would take
as long as doing a new episode."

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.710
Though neither Harmon nor Roiland offered
explicit details about the episode that never

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:20.581
was, Harmon noted that fans would flip over
the footage they have tucked away over at

00:01:20.581 --> 00:01:24.369
Adult Swim — but probably wouldn't actually
like the episode if it was ever completed

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:25.980
and included on a season of the show.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:26.840
Harmon said:

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.220
"Fans would love to watch that.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.880
They wouldn't love it as much as [an episode]
from season 4, but second to that.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:35.829
[...] And I anticipate that when presented
in that context fans would say, 'I love that,

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:36.829
why don't you make that?'

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:39.540
But I would say that if we broadcast it that
you would think it sucked."

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.760
Roiland then joked that he and Harmon would
crowdsource funding for fans to fix the rejected

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:47.780
Rick and Morty episode, because, as Harmon
said, if the animatic is out there, people

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:49.480
will find a way to animate it in full.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:53.020
It's hard to say what the scrapped episode
could have been about — especially since

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.859
the show's writers seems to be given permission
to take the show in pretty much any direction,

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:58.680
and nothing is considered totally off-limits.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:01:59.820
Roiland explained:

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.120
"We don't have any rules like that that I
can think of.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.240
One philosophy we tend to adopt is to keep
moving forward with new ideas, new worlds,

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.010
and not look back as often as other shows
might, just in fear of that coming off as

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:11.500
disingenuous fan service.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:12.740
But there's nothing 'banned.'"

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.560
Whether this missing Rick and Morty episode
will ever be unveiled remains a mystery.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:20.940
But Rick and Morty fans are endlessly passionate
— after all, these are the same people who

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:24.810
managed to help push McDonald's into bringing
back its limited-time-only Szechuan Sauce

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.440
after it was mentioned in the series.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:34.100
"I want that Mulan sauce Morty.  That's my series arch, I want my McNuggets Szechuan Sauce, Morty."

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.180
With that in mind, there should be little
doubt that they'd also figure out a way to

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.440
get the unmade episode animated and released.

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:43.400
Until that miracle happens, however, those
same fans will have to pass the time wondering

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:46.700
what that rejected episode could have been
about — and looking forward to the show's

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.620
fourth season, which premieres in November
2019.

